/**
 * @file nodus/tests/test_hf9_kat.c
 * @brief HF-9 T-4, C side, for what WP2 implements: this build's code
 *        against the independent oracle's groups "leg_vectors" and
 *        "leg_row_sets" of nodus/tests/vectors/hf9_kat.json.
 *
 * Plan docs/plans/approved/2026-11-04-hf9.md §12 row T-4, §4.1, §4.2, §15
 * AD-1 / AD-2 / AD-3 (A1, A2, A3, F-2, F-5, F-7).
 *
 * KAT FILE. nodus/tests/vectors/hf9_kat.json, sha256
 * 5cdd9c46372cc0c79a30eb4edc6aec25e23e4830d256a4a69afd2a85bb7e12fa
 * (checked at run time; a different file fails the test before anything
 * else), generated by nodus/tests/vectors/hf9_kat_oracle.py (merged into
 * this branch at a0167d82b; author is not the author of the HF-9 C code).
 * The file is read at run time with json-c, as test_roots_v2.c does for
 * archive_reward_kat.json; the path comes from CMake (HF9_KAT_PATH). The
 * oracle's PROVENANCE labels the trie structure and roots GROUNDED (the
 * pinned execution-specs merkle_patricia_trie.py is parsed and executed
 * with the plan's substitutions) and every other byte layout
 * SELF-CONSISTENT (the plan text and the existing C leaf layouts, not an
 * external reference).
 *
 * ── WHAT IT PROVES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  "leg_vectors" (8, one per leg) — for each leg, every value is produced
 *  by the C, never read back from the JSON's intermediate fields:
 *   - the key bytes (blobs raw, names ASCII, k / epoch_start 8-byte
 *     big-endian built here from the record's integers) equal the JSON
 *     key; the path SHA3-512(key) (qgp_sha3_512) equals the JSON path;
 *   - the leaf value from the production leaf function of the leg
 *     (nodus_witness_merkle_leaf_hash + the outer SHA3-512(0x00 ‖ D),
 *     dna_v2_token_leaf_hash, dna_claims_leaf_hash, dna_v2_name_leaf_hash,
 *     dna_v2_storage_node_leaf_hash, dna_v2_segment_leaf_hash_v2,
 *     dna_v2_vset_leaf_hash, dna_v2_attendance_leaf_hash) equals the JSON
 *     value; SHA3-512 of the JSON value_preimage equals it too (a control
 *     on the file's own consistency, not on the C);
 *   - L1 (A2 / F-5): the 340-byte preimage assembled here from the record
 *     fields equals the JSON preimage340, its SHA3-512 and the production
 *     D equal inner_digest_D, SHA3-512(0x00 ‖ D) equals the trie value
 *     and D is NOT the value; the short-owner variant (owner "abcdef0123456789",
 *     NUL-padded to 128 bytes) is rebuilt from its 340 bytes, hashed by the
 *     production function and compared with its D and trie value;
 *   - the single-leaf trie: root and root-node RLP (135 bytes) produced by
 *     the C incremental trie (evm_trie_*, an in-memory node store) and the
 *     root by evm_trie_full_root equal the JSON's;
 *   - legs 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8 additionally through the PRODUCTION reader and
 *     build path: the record is written into a scratch S20 database, and
 *     nodus_witness_v2_trie_build_leg returns root_inc == root_full == the
 *     JSON single-leaf root (so the SQL columns, the row reader, the key
 *     convention, the trie store and the full-rebuild cross-check agree
 *     with the oracle).
 *  "leg_row_sets" (48 sets: 8 legs x n = 0, 1, 2, 3, 16 and a 2-row
 *  extension case; 192 rows): every row's path equals SHA3-512(key) from
 *  the C; the keys are strictly ascending; the root by the C incremental
 *  trie fed in ascending key order, in descending order, and by
 *  evm_trie_full_root all equal the JSON root; the root node kind (empty,
 *  leaf, branch, extension) read from the C-produced root-node RLP equals
 *  the JSON label; n = 0 is the empty root SHA3-512(0x80) (A3 / F-7).
 *
 * GROUPS NOT CHECKED HERE (they belong to later packages and are named so
 * that nothing reads this test as covering them): new_tags, compositions
 * (WP3, plan §6, §7); hrw (WP4, §8.3); composition_chains,
 * empty_leg_proofs, merkle_steps (WP6, §10); votes (D6, WP3, §7.1). The
 * vector_counts of those groups are not asserted.
 *
 * ── FLAGS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  register_witness_test (NODUS_WITNESS_INTERNAL_API); only with
 *  NODUS_EVM_ENABLED (nodus_witness_v2_trie.c) and json-c (the test is not
 *  registered without it: that is missing coverage, never a pass; if the
 *  file is compiled without HF9_KAT_PATH it stops with #error). No
 *  environment, no fault injection.
 *
 * ── LEAVES BEHIND ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *  /tmp/test_hf9_kat_XXXXXX, removed at exit (a crash leaves it).
 *
 * ── HOW IT CAN FALSELY PASS OR SKIP ────────────────────────────────────
 *  - SELF-CONSISTENCY: the leaf layouts the C and the oracle share were
 *    both written from the plan text and the existing C layouts (the
 *    oracle labels them SELF-CONSISTENT). A green run proves the C equals
 *    the oracle's reading, NOT that the layouts are sound; only the trie
 *    structure and the roots over given (key, value) pairs are grounded in
 *    the pinned reference.
 *  - The leg-5 registry and leg-6 segment records do NOT go through the
 *    SQL reader: the oracle's registry fixture has node_fp that is not
 *    SHA3-512(node_pk) (the reader refuses such a row, by design) and a
 *    single segment k = 2 is not the contiguous 1..n the build scan
 *    requires. Their values and roots are produced through the production
 *    LEAF functions and the trie primitives only; the readers of legs 5
 *    and 6 are covered by test_hf9_trie_legs (against its own oracle).
 *  - The row sets carry key / path / value but no record: their values
 *    are the JSON's, so they check the trie structure and roots, the path
 *    hash and the ordering — not the leaf functions (those are the
 *    leg_vectors).
 *  - The in-memory node store is this test's fixture; the SQLite store is
 *    exercised by the leg_vectors SQL path and by test_hf9_trie_legs.
 *  - A KAT file regenerated with the same sha256 is the same file; a
 *    regenerated file fails the hash check, which is intended (update the
 *    recorded hash with the new oracle run).
 */

